DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmppttuuttiill utility can be used to display or modify various parameters
     on LSI Fusion-MPT controllers.  Each invocation of mmppttuuttiill consists of
     zero or more global options followed by a command.  Commands may support
     additional optional or required arguments after the command.

     Currently one global option is supported:

     --uu _u_n_i_t
             _u_n_i_t specifies the unit of the controller to work with.  If no
             unit is specified, then unit 0 is used.

     Volumes may be specified in two forms.  First, a volume may be identified
     by its location as [_x_x:]_y_y where _x_x is the bus ID and _y_y is the target
     ID.  If the bus ID is omitted, the volume is assumed to be on bus 0.
     Second, on the volume may be specified by the corresponding _d_a_X device,
     such as _d_a_0.

     The mpt(4) controller divides drives up into two categories.  Configured
     drives belong to a RAID volume either as a member drive or as a hot
     spare.  Each configured drive is assigned a unique device ID such as 0 or
     1 that is show in sshhooww ccoonnffiigg, and in the first column of sshhooww ddrriivveess.
     Any drive not associated with a RAID volume as either a member or a hot
     spare is a standalone drive.  Standalone drives are visible to the oper‐
     ating system as SCSI disk devices.  As a result, drives may be specified
     in three forms.  First, a configured drive may be identified by its de‐
     vice ID.  Second, any drive may be identified by its location as _x_x:_y_y
     where _x_x is the bus ID and _y_y is the target ID for each drive as dis‐
     played in sshhooww ddrriivveess.  Note that unlike volumes, a drive location always
     requires the bus ID to avoid confusion with device IDs.  Third, a stand‐
     alone drive that is not part of a volume may be identified by its corre‐
     sponding _d_a_X device as displayed in sshhooww ddrriivveess.

     The mmppttuuttiill utility supports several different groups of commands.  The
     first group of commands provide information about the controller, the
     volumes it manages, and the drives it controls.  The second group of com‐
     mands are used to manage the physical drives attached to the controller.
     The third group of commands are used to manage the logical volumes man‐
     aged by the controller.  The fourth group of commands are used to manage
     the drive configuration for the controller.

     The informational commands include:

     vveerrssiioonn
             Displays the version of mmppttuuttiill.

     sshhooww aaddaapptteerr
             Displays information about the RAID controller such as the model
             number.

     sshhooww ccoonnffiigg
             Displays the volume and drive configuration for the controller.
             Each volume is listed along with the physical drives that the
             volume spans.  If any hot spare drives are configured, then they
             are listed as well.

     sshhooww ddrriivveess
             Lists all of the physical drives attached to the controller.

     sshhooww eevveennttss
             Display all the entries from the controller's event log.  Due to
             lack of documentation this command is not very useful currently
             and just dumps each log entry in hex.

     sshhooww vvoolluummeess
             Lists all of the logical volumes managed by the controller.

     The physical drive management commands include:

     ffaaiill _d_r_i_v_e
             Mark _d_r_i_v_e as “failed requested”.  Note that this state is dif‐
             ferent from the “failed” state that is used when the firmware
             fails a drive.  _D_r_i_v_e must be a configured drive.

     oonnlliinnee _d_r_i_v_e
             Mark _d_r_i_v_e as an online drive.  _D_r_i_v_e must be part a configured
             drive in either the “offline” or “failed requested” states.

     oofffflliinnee _d_r_i_v_e
             Mark _d_r_i_v_e as offline.  _D_r_i_v_e must be a configured, online drive.

     The logical volume management commands include:

     nnaammee _v_o_l_u_m_e _n_a_m_e
             Sets the name of _v_o_l_u_m_e to _n_a_m_e.

     vvoolluummee ccaacchhee _v_o_l_u_m_e _e_n_a_b_l_e_|_e_n_a_b_l_e_d_|_d_i_s_a_b_l_e_|_d_i_s_a_b_l_e_d
             Enables or disables the drive write cache for the member drives
             of _v_o_l_u_m_e.

     vvoolluummee ssttaattuuss _v_o_l_u_m_e
             Display more detailed status about a single volume including the
             current progress of a rebuild operation if one is being per‐
             formed.

     The configuration commands include:

     cclleeaarr   Delete the entire configuration including all volumes and spares.
             All drives will become standalone drives.

     ccrreeaattee _t_y_p_e [--qq] [--vv] [--ss _s_t_r_i_p_e___s_i_z_e] _d_r_i_v_e[,_d_r_i_v_e[,...]]
             Create a new volume.  The _t_y_p_e specifies the type of volume to
             create.  Currently supported types include:

             rraaiidd00   Creates one RAID0 volume spanning the drives listed in
                     the single drive list.

             rraaiidd11   Creates one RAID1 volume spanning the drives listed in
                     the single drive list.

             rraaiidd11ee  Creates one RAID1E volume spanning the drives listed in
                     the single drive list.

             NNoottee:: Not all volume types are supported by all controllers.

             If the --qq flag is specified after _t_y_p_e, then a “quick” initial‐
             ization of the volume will be done.  This is useful when the
             drives do not contain any existing data that need to be pre‐
             served.

             If the --vv flag is specified after _t_y_p_e, then more verbose output
             will be enabled.  Currently this just provides notification as
             drives are added to volumes when building the configuration.

             The --ss _s_t_r_i_p_e___s_i_z_e parameter allows the stripe size of the array
             to be set.  By default a stripe size of 64K is used.  The list of
             valid values for a given _t_y_p_e are listed in the output of sshhooww
             aaddaapptteerr.

     ddeelleettee _v_o_l_u_m_e
             Delete the volume _v_o_l_u_m_e.  Member drives will become standalone
             drives.

     aadddd _d_r_i_v_e [_v_o_l_u_m_e]
             Mark _d_r_i_v_e as a hot spare.  _D_r_i_v_e must not be a member of a vol‐
             ume.  If _v_o_l_u_m_e is specified, then the hot spare will be dedi‐
             cated to that volume.  Otherwise, _d_r_i_v_e will be used as a global
             hot spare backing all volumes for this controller.  Note that
             _d_r_i_v_e must be as large as the smallest drive in all of the vol‐
             umes it is going to back.

     rreemmoovvee _d_r_i_v_e
             Remove the hot spare _d_r_i_v_e from service.  It will become a stand‐
             alone drive.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Mark the drive at bus 0 target 4 as offline:

           mmppttuuttiill oofffflliinnee 00::44

     Create a RAID1 array from the two standalone drives _d_a_1 and _d_a_2:

           mmppttuuttiill ccrreeaattee rraaiidd11 ddaa11,,ddaa22

     Mark standalone drive _d_a_3 as a global hot spare:

           mmppttuuttiill aadddd ddaa33

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     mpt(4)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The mmppttuuttiill utility first appeared in FreeBSD 8.0.

BBUUGGSS
     The handling of spare drives appears to be unreliable.  The mpt(4)
     firmware manages spares via spare drive “pools”.  There are eight pools
     numbered 0 through 7.  Each spare drive can only be assigned to a single
     pool.  Each volume can be backed by any combination of zero or more spare
     pools.  The mmppttuuttiill utility attempts to use the following algorithm for
     managing spares.  Global spares are always assigned to pool 0, and all
     volumes are always backed by pool 0.  For dedicated spares, mmppttuuttiill as‐
     signs one of the remaining 7 pools to each volume and assigns dedicated
     drives to that pool.  In practice however, it seems that assigning a
     drive as a spare does not take effect until the box has been rebooted.
     Also, the firmware renumbers the spare pool assignments after a reboot
     which undoes the effects of the algorithm above.  Simple cases such as
     assigning global spares seem to work ok (albeit requiring a reboot to
     take effect) but more “exotic” configurations may not work reliably.

     Drive configuration commands result in an excessive flood of messages on
     the console.

     The mpt version 1 API that is used by mmppttuuttiill and mpt(4) does not support
     volumes above two terabytes.  This is a limitation of the API.  If you
     are using this adapter with volumes larger than two terabytes, use the
     adapter in JBOD mode.  Utilize geom(8), zfs(8), or another software vol‐
     ume manager to work around this limitation.

